{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785732777,"nonce":"yym1AebRLVYp2CYr2s5s0Nhlo8M=","message":"**Digest choice is part of this ADR's remit — decide it, do not inherit it.** Added to ADR-0066 as point 11 (2026-08-03).\n\nScarab uses **SHA-256** (`sha2`, in `scarab-storage`'s pure-domain allow-block per ADR-0029/0031). **BLAKE3 appears nowhere in the workspace.** ADR-0066 declined to adopt it now — verified streaming (`bao`) is not what per-file laziness needs, re-addressing collides with the durability/ledger/drain-record work, and dual-hashing recreates the \"one address, two canonicalisations\" hazard closed on 2026-08-01.\n\n**But ADR-0066 point 11 fixed the escape hatch so this ticket can choose freely:** bare hex means SHA-256, and **any future digest gets its own tag** (`blake3:\u003chex\u003e`, or its own prefix namespace). Adoption is therefore ADDITIVE — new objects get tagged addresses, existing objects keep working, nothing is rewritten.\n\n**The specific question for this ADR:** packing means fetching **ranges of packs**. Do we want to verify a partial pack read WITHOUT extracting and re-hashing the individual blobs? That is the one genuine `bao` use case in this codebase.\n\n- The SHA-256 answer: fetch the range, extract the blob, hash it, compare to its address. Works today, costs a full re-hash of whatever you extracted.\n- The BLAKE3/`bao` answer: verify the range incrementally against a tree hash, without extracting. Cheaper on partial reads of large objects; irrelevant for the small-file case that dominates CI.\n\nEvaluate `bao-tree` on its own rather than pulling in `iroh` (which drags a QUIC/networking model along) — that was the 2026-08-03 library survey's verdict.\n\n- [ ] The packing ADR states its digest choice explicitly, with the partial-verification question answered either way\n- [ ] If BLAKE3 is tagged in, ADR-0066 point 11's tag convention is the mechanism — do not invent a second one","files":null}]}